Yes. The ugly can be unusually information-dense because it often exposes where something failed, what was neglected, what was distorted, or what had to be sacrificed.

Beauty often shows successful integration. Ugliness can reveal the seams.

A broken building teaches load paths. A bad interface reveals cognitive friction. A dysfunctional institution exposes incentives. A scar shows where force exceeded tolerance. A terrible story can make structure more visible because you can feel exactly where it loses you. Even an ugly emotion can reveal a boundary, unmet need, fear, attachment, or contradiction that prettier states conceal.

That does not make ugliness inherently wise or good. Some ugly things are simply destructive. But as an object of study, ugliness can have high diagnostic value.

There’s also a useful three-way distinction:

The good teaches what to preserve.
The beautiful teaches what can harmonize.
The ugly teaches what breaks, corrupts, scars, or refuses to fit.

And sometimes the ugliest cases are the best stress tests. If a principle only works in clean examples, you may not understand the principle yet.

In your terms: don’t only study the successful instantiation. Study the deformation. The delta between what the thing was supposed to become and what it actually became can reveal the hidden variables.

Maybe that gives us another compact rule:

Beauty shows the form. Ugliness shows the forces.